ty to do it and get away with it. Mr. Carruthers, don't be surprised, celebrities can be evil too. Although I must admit, I don't believe Nancy would do such a thing. We met when she was very well-mannered, and I don't think she could change so quickly. She was a brave woman then.\" [1094] I didn't say anything. I was thinking that the problem was that some killers had manners most of the time, but only needed to discard them once to commit murder. [1095] Margaret said, \"I didn't sleep last night, thinking it might be Walter Sturridge. No, it's not possible. He couldn't even stand up without help, let alone go to London. Killing three people is far beyond his abilities.\" [1096] \"Walter Stuart?\" I leaned forward in my chair and said, \"The old drunk I talked to at the King's Head last night? Why would he want to kill Harriet Sippey, Ada Gransbury, and Richard Nix?\" [1097] \"Because Francis Ives is his daughter.\" Margaret turned back to the window and looked at the tombstones of the Ives couple. Suddenly, Shakespeare's lines from the Sonnets came to my mind: \"Rumour's a dish that hath been long in the stew, and hath been dress'd and seasoned with old wives' tongues.\" [1098] \"I would be glad if Walter killed them,\" Margaret said, \"am I terrible? As long as it's not Nancy, I'm fine. Walter is old, and I think he doesn't have much time left. Oh, I don't want it to be Nancy! I read in the newspaper how successful she was as an artist. She made a name for herself after leaving here, which is a comfort to me. Whenever I think of her succeeding in London, I feel happy.\" [1099] \"Leave here?\" I said, \"Did Nancy Drew used to live in Gleyholling too?\" [1100] Margaret stared out the window and said, \"Yes, until 1913.\" [1101] \"The year Patric and Frances Ives died and Richard Nixes left the village.\" [1102] \"Yes.\" [1103] \"Margaret\u2026\" I leaned forward, trying to get her attention away from the Ives' graves. \"I sincerely hope my arrival has convinced you