ges' face contorted in agony, and he couldn't continue. [648] \"You don't have to blame yourself, Mr. Nigges,\" Poirot said. \"Your brother's death was not your fault.\" [649] \"But I can't help but think\u2026 something terrible must have happened to him in that village. People usually avoid talking about such things when they're awful.\" [650] \"Richard didn't want to talk about it, so I assumed it was best not to ask. You see, he was the eldest brother, more respected, and everyone listened to him. He was also very intelligent, you know?\" [651] \"Oh, before Richard became depressed, no one could be as meticulous as him. He was always careful and precise in everything he did. You could trust him, and everyone did. That's why he became a good lawyer, until things took a turn for the worse. I always thought he would pull himself together. A few months ago, when I saw his spirits lift, I thought 'Richard has finally regained his love for life' and hoped he would start working again before he spent all his savings\u2026\"\n[652] \"Mr. Nigges, can you slow down?\" Poirot said, politely but firmly. \"Your brother didn't have a job when he moved to your house?\" [653] \"No. When Richard came to Devonshire, he left Gleeholm and Ada Gransbury behind, as well as his job. He stopped practicing law and spent his days locked in his room, drinking alone.\" [654] \"Ah, what do you mean by 'depressed'?\" [655] \"Well,\" Nigges said, \"Richard, who lived with me, was a completely different person from the one I had seen before. He was silent, gloomy, and seemed to have built a wall around himself. He didn't go out, see anyone, write to anyone, or receive any letters. He just read and stared into space. He refused to join us for church and even refused to be a little more polite to my wife. One day, a year after he moved in, I found a Bible on the floor outside his room. It was a gift we had given him, which had been in his bedroom drawer. I wanted to put it back, but Richard made