d life again.\" [666] \"Curse, sir? Do you think the village is cursed?\" [667] Henry Nigges' face flushed. \"Not a real curse, of course not. There wouldn't be such a thing, would there? It was my wife who said it, and she's not well-educated, so she came up with that word. Given Richard's eagerness to leave the village, breaking off the engagement, and the other things she knows, it's not surprising.\" [668] \"What other things?\" [669] \"Ah, I see you don't know about that, do you? How could you? It happened to the young vicar and his wife who took care of the parish. Richard told us in a letter a few months before he left the village that the vicar and his wife died within hours of each other.\" [670] \"Really? What was the cause of death?\" Poirot asked. [671] \"I don't know, even if Richard knew, he didn't explain it in detail in the letter. He only told us that it was a terrible tragedy. In fact, I asked him again later, but he yelled at me, and I still don't know. I think he was too immersed in his own misfortunes, unwilling to care about other people's affairs.\" [672] * * *\n[673] [1] A historical city in West Africa, Mali, often used to describe a very distant place. [674] Chapter Eight\n[675] Summarizing the situation\n[676] Half an hour later, Polo and I left \"The Happy Coffee Shop\" and headed back to our accommodations. On the way, he said, \"If everything goes as planned, the unpleasant events of sixteen years ago are interconnected: the tragic fate of the priest and his wife, Richard Nix's sudden breakup with Ada Gransbury, Richard Nix's aversion to Gleghorn, and his decision to flee, finally arriving in Devonshire, where he spent his days drinking, being idle, and squandering his fortune, almost dying in his brother's house!\" [677] \"Do you think Richard Nix's drinking was related to the priest's death?\" I asked. \"It's certainly intriguing to connect everything, but is it also possible that these events have no connection at all?\" [678] \"No, I don't think so,\" Polo shot me a look. \"Kitcher, breathing some fresh air under the winter sun helps oxygenate your gray brain cells. My friend, take a deep breath.\" [679] I took a breath as he instructed, which amused him. Of course, I had to breathe, so I looked silly. [680] \"Alright. Now think about this: the young priest not only died tragically, but it was even more unusual that he died just hours after his wife's death. Richard Nix told his brother about it in a letter, and a few months later, he broke off his engagement with Ada Gransbury. Then he fled to Devonshire and led a debauched life. He didn't want to see the Bible in his room, nor did he go to church, even to comfort the mistress.\" [681] \"Do these have any special meaning?\" I asked him. [682] \"Ha! It takes some effort for oxygen to reach the brain cells! It doesn't matter, it will eventually reach where it needs to go - the size of a needle case in your brain. Ketchup, the church! Richard Nigges started to hate the village, the church, and the Bible after the tragic death of the vicar and his wife.\" [683] \"Oh, I know what you're thinking.\" [684] \"Good. Richard Nigges returned to Devonshire and lived a degenerate life for years. However, during this time, his brother never interfered in his affairs, otherwise he might have saved him from the abyss of self-destruction.\" [685] \"Do you think Henry Nigges was too careless in this regard?\" [686] \"It's not his fault,\" Poirot waved his hand, \"He is British. Even if you see disasters that could be avoided, you sit politely and quietly without getting involved!\" [687] \"I think that's unfair of you to say.\u201d As we walked through the bustling streets of London, I had to raise my voice. [688] Poirot ignored my complaint. \"For years, Henry Nigges has been silently concerned about his brother. He was full of hope and prayed for him. And just when he was about to lose hope, his prayers seemed to be answered - Richard Nigges' mental state improved a few months ago, and it seemed like he was planning something. Considering what we have learned, perhaps his plan also included booking rooms at the Brooks Han Hotel for himself and the two women he met in Glehling. Then, last night, he died at the Brooks Han Hotel, with a cufflink engraved with a letter combination in his mouth. His former fianc\u00e9e, Ada Gransbury, and his former neighbor, Harriet Sipper, were also killed, lying next to him, in the same way.\" [689] Poirot suddenly stopped. He had walked too fast and was slightly out of breath. \"Kagirpur,\" he panted, taking out a neatly folded handkerchief from his waistcoat pocket and wiping the sweat from his forehead, \"Which of these events do you think was the beginning of everything? Wasn't it the tragic death of the priest and his wife?\" [690] \"Oh, yes, but we need to determine if this is related to the triple murder at the Brooks Han Hotel. Poirot, we have no evidence. I still think that the poor priest had nothing to do with these things.\" [691] \"Isn't that the same for poor Jenny?\" [692] \"Yes.\" [693] We continued walking down the street. [694] \"Poirot, have you ever played crossword puzzles? Because...oh, do you know I'm designing a crossword puzzle of my own?\" [695] \"It's hard not to know, my friend, living so close to you.\" [696] \"Yes, that's true. Well, I discovered something interesting while thinking about the clues. For example, the clue is 'kitchen utensil, three letters', and the first letter given is 'p'. The thought is simple, it's 'pot', a three-letter word starting with 'p' that is a kitchen utensil. You would think it's definitely the right answer, but the correct answer is 'pan', another three-letter word starting with 'p'. Do you understand what I'm saying?\" [697] \"Carter, your example doesn't fit well. Based on your description, I would guess that both 'pot' and 'pan' could be the answer. Only a fool would think of just one.\" [698] \"Okay. If you want another equally plausible explanation, how about this: Richard Nigges refuses to go to church and doesn't want the Bible in his room, indicating that he encountered misfortune in Grellingholme that eroded his faith. Doesn't that explanation sound perfect too? And the thing that happened to him might have nothing to do with the death of the clergy couple. Richard Nigges is not the first person to find himself in despair and question whether God loves him as much as he loves others!\" [699] \"Have you ever questioned it, Carter?\" Poirot pulled on my sleeve, stopping me from walking any further. I always forget that my legs are longer than Poirot's. [700] \"Honestly, I have questioned it, but I still go to church for services. But I know some people are affected by it.\" Those who get defensive when their intelligence is called into question, rather than remaining silent. But to Poirot, I said, \"I think it depends on whether you want to take responsibility for your own problems or let God take responsibility.\" [701] \"Is your dilemma related to a woman?\" [702] \"There are several good girls, and my parents eagerly hope that I will marry one of them, but I firmly refuse to choose any of them.\" [703] Poirot quickly caught up with me and said, \"So, according to your understanding, we must forget the dead clergy couple? We must pretend not to know about it, so as not to be misled and reach a wrong conclusion? And the same goes for Jenny, right?\" [704] \"Oh, no, I just said that it's not right to do so. I didn't say to forget, but\u2026\"\n[705] \"Let me tell you what's right to do! You must go to Grell Holling. Harryt Westper, Ida Gransberry, and Richard Nix are not three fill-in-the-blanks, nor are they three things. Let's work together to put them together. Before they were killed, they were real people with blood and emotions: sometimes doing silly things, sometimes being wise and far-sighted. Kacherpul, you must go to the village where they once lived and find out who they really are.\" [706] \"Me? Or us?\" [707] \"No, my friend, Poirot will stay in London. To make progress in the case, I only need to think, not move my body.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[2248] \"You also told me that Ada or Harriet said something else, but you can't remember. Then the person you thought was Richard Nigg said, 'Does he have a brain? I don't think he does. I don't agree with what you just said about 'old enough to be his mother.' At that point, the woman called Harriet smiled and said, 'Well, we can't prove who's right, can we? Let's agree to disagree.' Is that right?\" [2249] Raphael Burbank confirmed Poirot's recollection was accurate. [2250] \"Good. Mr. Burbank, I believe that the line you can't remember was said by Harriet, and I'm one hundred percent sure that in that room, you didn't hear Ada Gransbury say a single word! And you didn't see her face because she was sitting with her back to the door.\" [2251] Baxter furrowed his brow, concentrated for a moment, and then said, \"I think you're right, Mr. Poirot. Yes, I didn't see Miss Edith Gransbury's face. And... after your reminder, I remember that I didn't hear her speak.\" [2252] \"Of course you didn't hear her speak, sir. The reason is simple: the Edith Gransbury you saw at 7:15, sitting with her back to the door and slumped in the chair, was already dead. There was a corpse in Room 317 when you brought the afternoon tea!\" [2253] Chapter 24\n[2254] The Blue Pot and Bowl\n[2255] There were a few screams of terror in the dining room, and I almost screamed myself. It was too strange! Thanks to my work at Scotland Yard, I've seen many corpses, but none were as terrifying as the sight of a dead woman sitting upright, as if she were enjoying tea with her friends. [2256] Poor Raphael Burbak trembled all over and his lips quivered uncontrollably. He had been so close to the monster that no sane person would have done the same. [2257] \"That's why the afternoon tea had to be delivered to Miss Ada Gransbury's room,\" Poirot continued. \"Room 238, where Mr. Richard Nigges lived, should have been the most convenient meeting place for the three victims, as it was between the other two rooms. And, without Mr. Nigges having to say anything, the afternoon tea would naturally be charged to his account. But they couldn't let Raphael Burbak see the three victims alive at seven fifteen in Room 238! If they had, they would have had to remove Miss Ada Gransbury's corpse from her room, carry it through the hotel corridor, and transport it to the lower-level Room 238. That would have been too risky and someone would have seen it.\" [2258] The faces of the bewildered people were now filled with shock. I imagine that Luca Lazzari might soon need to hire new staff. I certainly don't want to come back to the Brawnsham Hotel after the case is over. I imagine that most of the people in this room must feel the same way. [2259] Poirot continued to explain: \"Ladies and gentlemen, please carefully recall Mr. Richard Nigges' generosity. Oh, he was so generous, insisting on paying for the tea and paying for a car to pick up Harriet and Ada from the station. Why didn't they come together on the train and then share a car to the hotel? Since Mr. Richard Nigges knew that he, Harriet, and Ada were all about to die, why did he care so much about whether the food and drink bills were charged to his name?\" [2260] This was a good question. Each point Poirot made was on target and something I should have thought of. Regardless, I hadn't noticed how many inconsistencies there were in Miss Hobbs' statement. How could I have overlooked so many obvious gaps? [2261] Poirot said, \"The person pretending to be Richard Nigges was only trying to make Rafael Borker see him at 7:15 and 7:30 to see Thomas Brine. He didn't care about the bills! He knew that he and his accomplices wouldn't have to pay and that he had taken the food outside. So, how did he take the food out? He put it in a suitcase! Canterbury, do you remember seeing a homeless man near the hotel when we took the bus? The homeless man was eating from a suitcase, do you remember? You said he was 'a homeless man with cream.' Tell me, did you see him eating cream clearly?\" [2262] \"Oh, my goodness! Yes, I saw! He was eating a piece... of cake with cream inside.\" [2263] Poirot nodded and said, \"He found a discarded suitcase near Brookes Han hotel that contained tea for three people! Now, my friend, let me test your memory again. Do you remember, when I first came to Brookes Han, you told me that Miss Edith Gransberry brought a lot of clothes, enough to fill the closet? But her room only had one suitcase, the same size as Richard Nigges's and Harriet Sipper's, but they brought fewer clothes. When I asked you to put Miss Gransberry's clothes in her suitcase this afternoon, what did you find?\" [2264] \"It didn't fit. \" I felt like a big fool again. I felt foolish earlier because I couldn't even organize Miss Edith Gransberry's luggage, and now there's another reason. [2265] \"You were blaming yourself,\" Poirot said. \"You always like to criticize yourself, but in reality, it's impossible to fit all the clothes in, because they were brought in two suitcases. Even Hercule Poirot couldn't fit them into one suitcase.\" [2266] He turned around and said to the people in the restaurant, \"It was while throwing away the suitcase full of food and returning to the hotel that the man met Thomas Brinell, the receptionist of Blauxham, who is the door of this restaurant. Why did he deliberately discuss the bill with Brinell? There is only one reason: to deepen Brinell's impression and make him remember that Richard Nigges was still alive at half past seven. But the man who impersonated Mr. Nigges made a mistake. He said, 'Nigges can afford it, while Harriet Sipper and Ada Gransbury can't.' But that's not true! Henry Nigges, Richard's brother, can prove that Richard has no income and has little left in savings. But the man playing Richard Nigges didn't know that. He thought Richard was a gentleman, a former lawyer, and must have a lot of money.\" [2267] \"Henry Nigges first told me and Ketaki that since moving to Devonshire, his brother Richard has become gloomy and preoccupied, like a recluse with no desire for life. Mr. Henry Nigges, am I right?\" [2268] \"Yes, that's right.\" [2269] \"A recluse! Would a recluse like sherry and cake, would he chat with two women in a luxurious hotel in London? No! The man who took the afternoon tea from Rafael Bocaccio and the sherry from Thomas Brinell was not Richard Nigges. That man complimented Mr. Brinell on his efficiency and said something like this: 'You're efficient, and I believe you can get the job done - charge the cost of food and drink to my account, Richard Nigges in room 238 at Blauxham.' He chose his words carefully to make Thomas Brinell believe this Richard Nigges, and they must have met before. Mr. Brinell may have felt guilty at the time because he couldn't remember meeting this Mr. Nigges before, so he decided to remember him this time. He wanted to remember that he had met this man at least twice. Of course, working in a big hotel in London, he has to meet hundreds of people every day! I'm sure there are often cases where he remembers their names and appearances, but they forget what they look like - for him, they're just 'guests'!\" [2270] \"Excuse me, Mr. Poirot, forgive me,\" Luca Lazzari rushed up and said, \"generally speaking, you are right, but there are exceptions. Thomas Brinell is an exception, he has a super memory and can remember guests' faces and names very well. He has a super memory!\" [2271] Poirot smiled approvingly and said, \"Is that so? Well, it seems I'm right.\" [2272] \"About what?\" I asked. [2273] \"Please be patient, Ketton-Cremer, I'll explain one by one. The day before the murder, on Wednesday, when Richard Nix checked into the hotel, the man impersonating him was in the lobby. He might have been casing the place for his upcoming impersonation. Anyway, he saw Richard Nix. How did he recognize Richard Nix? I'll get to that in a moment. He saw Thomas Breen check him in and hand him the room key. The next evening, after the man impersonating Nix took the afternoon tea and disposed of the food outside, on his way back to Room 317, he met Thomas Breen. He was a quick-witted man who immediately saw an excellent opportunity to further mislead the police. He approached Breen, greeted him, and claimed to be Richard Nix, reminding Breen that they had met before. [2274] \"In fact, Thomas Breen had never met this man before, but he remembered the name Richard Nix because he had given him the room key. When suddenly someone spoke to him in a confident, friendly, and intimate manner, claiming to be Richard Nix, Thomas Breen naturally believed he was Richard Nix. Although he couldn't remember the face, he would only blame himself for being careless.\" [2275] Thomas Breen's face turned as red as wine. [2276] Poirot continued, \"The imposter who pretended to be Richard Nigges ordered another sherry. Why? To prolong his meeting with Thomas Brineau and make him remember him? Or to calm his nerves with alcohol? Both are possible.\" [2277] \"Now, allow me to change the subject. We found cyanide in the remaining sherry, just like in the cups of Miss Harriet Sipper and Miss Ada Gransbury. However, it was not the tea or sherry that killed these three victims. It couldn't have been. The murders had already occurred when the drinks were delivered to the rooms. The poisoned sherry glass and two tea cups were placed on the small table next to the body to create a false crime scene, leading us to believe the incident occurred after 7:15 p.m. In fact, Miss Harriet Sipper, Miss Ada Gransbury, and Mr. Richard Nigges were poisoned earlier using a different method. There is a glass cup by the sink in each room, isn't there, Mr. Lazari?\" [2278] \"Yes, Mr. Poirot, there is one in each room.\" [2279] \"So, I think they must have ingested the poison \u2013 in water. Afterward, the killer cleaned the glass that held the poison and placed it back by the sink. Mr. Brineau! Poirot suddenly called out, causing the reception assistant to shrink his neck, as if someone had shot him, \"You don't like talking in public, but when we first gathered in this restaurant, you mustered the courage to tell us that you had encountered Mr. Nigges in the corridor. But you didn't mention the sherry, even though I specifically asked about it. Later, you came to me and added the sherry incident. I asked you why you didn't mention it before, and you didn't answer. I didn't understand the reason at the time, but my friend, Caderousse, said some very insightful words that inspired me. He said you are a responsible person, and now involved in a murder case, you are concealing something that makes you very embarrassed and you are sure it has nothing to do with the case. He was right on the mark, wasn't he?\" [2280] Mr. Brineau nodded slightly. [2281] \"Allow me to explain,\" Poirot raised his voice, even though it was already quite loud, \"When we gathered in this restaurant last time, I asked everyone if they had delivered sherry to Mr. Nigus' room. No one spoke. Why didn't Thomas Brine say, 'I didn't deliver it to his room, but I did bring him a sherry'? Poirot will tell you! He didn't because he had doubts and didn't want to risk saying something uncertain. [2282] \"Among all the hotel staff, only Mr. Brine has seen one of the three victims \u2013 or rather, he was misled to believe he had seen Richard Nigus more than once. He knows he gave a man who claimed to be Richard Nigus a sherry, and the man acted as if he had seen him before, but he didn't look like the Richard Nigus he remembered. Don't forget, Mr. Lazari told us that Mr. Brine has excellent memory for names and faces. That's why he didn't speak when I asked about the sherry! He was thinking it through. A voice whispered in his head: 'It must be him, the same person. But it's not him, I can recognize him.'\" [2283] \"Thank God,\" said Thomas Brine. \"Poirot, every word you say is true.\" [2284] \"Let me explain further,\" Poirot raised his voice again, \"When we gathered in this restaurant last time, I asked everyone if they had delivered sherry to Mr. Nigus' room. No one spoke. Why didn't Thomas Brine say, 'I didn't deliver it to his room, but I did bring him a sherry'? Poirot will tell you! He didn't because he had doubts and didn't want to risk saying something uncertain. [2285] \"Among all the hotel staff, only Mr. Brine has seen one of the three victims \u2013 or rather, he was misled to believe he had seen Richard Nigus more than once. He knows he gave a man who claimed to be Richard Nigus a sherry, and the man acted as if he had seen him before, but he didn't look like the Richard Nigus he remembered. Don't forget, Mr. Lazari told us that Mr. Brine has excellent memory for names and faces. That's why he didn't speak when I asked about the sherry\n[2286] \"Of course,\" Poirot said without modesty. \"Please remember, ladies and gentlemen, the same name does not necessarily mean the same person. When Mr. Lazarus described the registration of Miss Jenny Hobbs to me, I also thought she was the same Miss Jenny Hobbs I had met at the 'Caf\u00e9 Joyeux.' It sounded similar, light hair, dark brown hat, light brown coat. But two men had only seen a woman fitting that description once, and they could not be sure they saw the same person. [2287] \"This point troubled me. I was already suspicious that the corpse of Richard Nigel I had seen at the crime scene and the living Richard Nigel that Mr. Raphael Barker and Mr. Thomas Brineau had seen on the night of the crime were two different men. Then I remembered that Mr. Thomas Brineau had checked in Mr. Richard Nigel on the Wednesday when he arrived at the Brawnsham Hotel. If I'm right, then this Richard Nigel is the real Richard Nigel. Suddenly, I understood Mr. Thomas Brineau's dilemma. How could he say in front of everyone that a man seemed to have two faces? They would think he was crazy! [2288] \"You're half crazy now, Mr. Poirot,\" Samuel Kidder sneered. [2289] Poirot ignored him and continued, \"The imposter may not look very similar to Richard, but I am certain he can imitate his voice very well. He is very good at imitation - Mr. Kidder, isn't he? [2290] \"Don't believe this man! He's a fraud!\" [2291] \"No, Mr. Kidder, you're the fraud. You've impersonated me more than once.\" [2292] At this point, Phyllis Springle, sitting in the back of the restaurant, stood up and said, \"You should all believe Mr. Poirot. He's telling the truth, and it's all fact. I've heard Samuel Kidder mimic his accent before. With my eyes closed, I couldn't tell the difference.\" [2293] \"Samuel Kidder doesn't just deceive people with his voice,\" Poirot said. \"When I first met him, he pretended to be a sloppy, intellectually challenged man. His shirt was stained, and he had lost a button. His beard was half-shaved. Mr. Kidder, tell these people why you went to such lengths to make yourself look so unkempt?\" [2294] Samuel Kidder stared resolutely ahead, saying nothing, his eyes filled with disgust. [2295] \"Well, if you don't want to say, I'll explain for you. Mr. Kidder escaped from the window of Richard Nigus's room 238 and climbed down. His face was scratched by the branches outside. A well-dressed, well-groomed man with a scratch on his face would be conspicuous and arouse suspicion, wouldn't he? A well-dressed person wouldn't leave a scar from a razor. Mr. Kidder didn't want me to think along these lines, didn't want me to guess that he had recently climbed out of a window and down a tree, so he created an unkempt appearance. He pretended he couldn't shave himself properly, that he would hurt himself if he tried, to avoid causing more scratches. Poirot would think it was only natural for such a sloppy man to cut himself with a razor. That's what I initially thought. \" [2296] \"Wait, Poirot,\" I said. \"If you say that Samuel Kid climbed out of Richard Nigges' window...\"\n[2297] \"Does it mean he killed Mr. Nigges? No, he didn't. He helped the murderer kill Richard Nigges. As for the murderer... I haven't gotten to that yet.\" [2298] \"Yes, you haven't gotten there yet,\" I said bluntly. \"You also didn't say who the three people were in Room 317 when Rafael Bacard sent the afternoon tea. You only said that the three victims were already dead at that time.\" [2299] \"Yes, at 7:15, the three people in Room 317 included the deceased Edith Gransbury. But she was placed on a chair, and if you didn't see her face, you would think she was still alive. The other one was Samuel Kid, who impersonated Richard Nigges.\" [2300] \"Yes, I guessed that, but who was the third person? The one who impersonated Harriet Sipper and talked about gossip? It couldn't have been Jane Hobbs. As you said, Jane was on her way to 'The Happy Coffee Shop' at that time.\" [2301] \"Ah, yes, the woman who loved to gossip,\" Poirot said. \"I'll tell you who she is, my friend. It was Nancy Duane.\" [2302] There was a chorus of shocked exclamations in the restaurant. [2303] \"Oh, no, Poirot sir,\" Luca Lazzari said.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIf you ask a doctor or a medical expert, theoretically, can a twelve or thirteen-year-old girl have a child? The answer is either 'yes' or 'no.' Let's not get caught up in the age at which a woman can have a child! Remember Samuel Kid's comment about the young man, it was quite amusing. He said, 'Does he have a brain? I don't think he does.' You would think that Kid's meaning was that the young man was a fool.\" [2441] \"Yes, that's exactly what I think,\" I said impatiently. \"Since you're so much smarter than me, why don't you just tell me where I'm wrong?\" [2442] Polo grumbled discontentedly. \"My goodness! The couple in Room 317 were Harriet Sipper and her husband George. It wasn't a serious debate, it was just joking. George Sipper died when they were both young, and Samuel Kid said he didn't have a brain, meaning that if he existed after death, he wouldn't be in human form, but in a soul, right? Souls don't have human brains, so George Sipper's soul couldn't have a brain either.\" [2443] \"I\u2026 oh, my goodness. Yes, now I understand.\" [2444] \"Samuel Kid began his argument with 'I think\u2026' because he knew that Nancy Duane would disagree with him. She might say, 'Souls certainly have ideas, and souls can be attached to people, don't they have free will? Where does their will come from if they don't have a brain?'\" [2445] From a philosophical perspective, this is an interesting point of view. In other circumstances, I might also express my own opinion.\" [2446] Poirot continued, \"Nancy said 'old enough to be his mother' because she believed that when a person dies, their age is fixed forever. In the afterlife, they won't age. If George Sipper's soul could come back to visit his wife, he would still be a twenty-something-year-old, the same age he was when he died. And she, as a forty-something-year-old woman, is now old enough to be his mother.\" [2447] \"Great!\" Jenny exclaimed sincerely, \"I wasn't there at the time, but they argued again in front of me later. Mr. Poirot, you are truly insightful. Mr. Kitchener, I hope you appreciate him.\" She then turned to Poirot and said, \"Their argument never ends...oh, forever! Nancy insists she's right, and Sem doesn't back down. He says souls are not limited by time dimensions, they don't have a concept of time, so you can't say someone is old enough to be a soul's mother.\" [2448] Poirot said to me, \"How disgusting, isn't it, Mr. Kitchener? When Rafael Buccar went to deliver the dessert, Nancy Drew sat next to the corpse of Ida Gransberry propped up on a chair, mocking another woman who died that day due to their conspiracy. Poor and foolish Harryette, her husband had no interest in communicating with her directly from the grave, he only wanted to talk to Jenny Hobbs. And Harryette had no choice, if she wanted to receive his message, she had to go to Bluxton to see Jenny. But going to see Jenny meant certain death.\" [2449] \"No one deserves to be killed more than Harryette Sipper.\" Jenny said, \"I regret many things, but I don't regret killing Harryette.\" [2450] \"What about Ida Gransberry?\" I asked, \"Why did she go to the Bluxton Hotel?\" [2451] \"Ah!\" Poirot said, always eager to share his seemingly endless knowledge with others, \"Ada also received an irresistible invitation from Richard Nigges. Not to speak with the deceased lover, but to meet her separated for sixteen years' ex-fianc\u00e9. It's not hard to imagine how tempting that is. Richard Nigges abandoned Ada, which undoubtedly broke her heart. She remained single for life. I think he hinted at reconciliation, even possibly marriage, in his letter. Ada agreed \u2013 who wouldn't give love another chance? Richard told her he would come to her room at Bluxton Hotel on Thursday from 3:30 to 4:00 pm. Ketteral, do you remember saying that you would go to the hotel on Wednesday so that you could spend Thursday preparing for the murder? Does it make more sense now?\" [2452] I nodded and said, \"Nigges knew he was going to commit murder on Thursday and that he would be killed himself. To prepare mentally for the double test, he naturally wanted to be on the scene a day earlier.\" [2453] \"Also to avoid any delays that could hinder the plan,\" Poirot said. [2454] \"So, Jane Hobbs killed Harriet Seppel, and Richard Nigges killed Ada Gransberry?\" I asked. [2455] \"Yes, my friend,\" Poirot said, looking at Jane, who nodded. \"I believe the murders in rooms 121 and 317 were carried out around the same time. I suspect the methods were the same, luring Harriet and Ada to drink poison. Jane told Harriet, 'I think you need to drink some water before I tell you what I have to say. Sit down, and I'll get you some water.' They took cups from the sink to fill with water, and they slipped in the poison. Then they handed the poisoned water to the victims, who soon died.\" [2456] \"How did Richard Naygreaves die?\" I asked. [2457] \"According to their previous plan, Jeanne was supposed to kill him,\" said Poirot. [2458] \"Most of what I said to you at Semple's was true,\" said Jeanne. \"Richard did write to me after many years. He was tormented by what he had done to Patrick and Francis. He couldn't find a way out, couldn't make amends, couldn't find inner peace, unless we all paid with our lives. We all had a responsibility.\" [2459] \"He made you... help him kill Harriet and Ida?\" I asked, trying to clarify the situation. [2460] \"Yes, they, and myself,\" said Jeanne. \"He insisted that we all had to die, otherwise it wouldn't have any meaning. He didn't want to be a murderer, he wanted to be an executor - he used that word several times, which means that neither of us could escape punishment. I agreed that Harriet and Ida should die, they were too evil. But... I didn't want to die, nor did I want Richard to die. I think what he did was sincere repentance for what he had done, and that was enough for Patrick, and for any higher power that might exist or not exist. But I couldn't convince Richard. I soon realized that there was no point in trying to convince him. He was as clever as ever, but he had lost some parts that made him paranoid, strange, and come up with many absurd ideas. He never forgot that event, those sins... He became a strange madman. I knew that if I didn't comply with his proposal, he would kill me. He didn't say it outright because he didn't want me to be coerced. You know, he was very good to me, he just needed an ally, someone who shared his views. He sincerely believed that I would agree to his plot because he thought I was more reasonable than Harriet and Ida. He was too confident in his own opinion, believing that only by sacrificing our four lives could he atone for his sins. I think he may have been right, but I was afraid. But now I'm not afraid anymore, and I don't know what changed me. Maybe, despite living in pain all these years, I still held a glimmer of hope that things would get better. Sadness does not equal despair.\" [2461] \"You knew you had to pretend to comply to save your life,\" said Poirot. \"Perfectly deceiving Richard Nix was the only way to escape death. But you didn't know how, so you turned to Nancy Drew.\" [2462] \"Yes, I went to her. She helped me, or so I thought. Her plan was brilliant. She suggested that I change one part of the plan. Richard's original plan was that once Harriet and Ada were dead, he would kill me and then commit suicide. Naturally, as someone who enjoyed being in control and being the authority, he wanted to be in charge from start to finish. [2463] \"Nancy told me to convince Richard that I should kill him, not the other way around. 'Impossible!' I said, 'He won't agree.' But Nancy said he would, as long as I presented it the right way. She advised me to act more obsessed with our goal than he was. She was right; it worked. I went to Richard and told him that it wasn't enough for just the four of us\u2014me, him, Harriet, and Ada\u2014to die. Nancy had to be punished too. I told him that Nancy was even more evil than Harriet, and how she had plotted to steal Patrick away from his wife and wouldn't stop until she succeeded. I also told him that Nancy had confessed to me that her speech at the King's Head Inn wasn't meant to help Patrick, but to hurt Francis. Nancy wanted Francis to commit suicide or at least give up Patrick and return to Cambridge to be with her father, so she could take his place.\" [2464] \"More lies,\" said Poirot. [2465] \"Yes, more lies\u2014but these were Nancy's ideas, and they worked! Richard agreed that I should die first.\" [2466] \"And he didn't know about Samuel Kid, did he?\" said Poirot. [2467] \"No, he didn't. Nancy and I brought in Sem, who was part of our plan. Neither Nancy nor I wanted to climb out the window, down the tree, and risk breaking our necks after locking the door and hiding the key behind the tiles. But that was the only way out of Room 238, so we needed Samuel's help. He not only did that but also impersonated Richard.\" [2468] \"Hiding the key behind the tiles,\" I murmured to myself, confirming the details in my mind. \"So, the story you told us at Mr. Kid's house was seamless, Richard Naylors hiding the key to make us think the killer took it, so he could frame Nancy Drew for the murder at the Blaux Han hotel.\" [2469] \"That's right,\" said Poirot. \"More precisely, he thought that was the case. When Jenny gave him the poisoned water, he still believed Jenny would live and ensure that Nancy Drew was accused of the murder at the Blaux Han Hotel. He believed Jenny would report to the police and make sure they suspected Nancy. But he didn't know that Nancy had prepared a solid alibi, that she was with the Wallace couple at that time! He also didn't know that a brooch would be placed in his mouth, that the key would be hidden behind the tiles, and that the windows would be open...\"\n[2470] \"Yes, Richard didn't know all of that,\" agreed Jenny. \"You now understand why I said Nancy's plan was brilliant, Mr. Poirot.\" [2471] \"She's a talented artist, madam. A clever artist who can see how to design details and also grasp the framework, knowing how to integrate all the elements together.\" [2472] Jenny turned to me and said, \"Nancy and I didn't want to do this, you have to believe me, Mr. Ketchup. If I had opposed Mr. Richard's plan, he would have killed me.\" She sighed. \"We planned it all, Nancy would escape unharmed, and Nancy and I would be punished for framing and accusing her, but not with the death penalty. We hoped to serve a few months in prison. After that, I planned to marry Sammy.\" [2473] \"Madam, everything was already ruined. I know you killed Harriet Sipper and Richard Nigges.\" [2474] \"I didn't kill Richard, Mr. Poirot. You're wrong about that.\" [2475] \"Yes, but you deceived him. Richard Nigges agreed to die because you agreed to participate in his plan, that all four of you would die. Then you brought Nancy Dunn into it, and the plan became five people. But you didn't really agree to his plan. You betrayed him and set up another trap behind his back. Who knows if Richard Nigges would have chosen to die in that way if he had known about your secret agreement with Nancy Dunn?\" [2476] Jane's expression stiffened. \"I didn't kill Richard Nigg. I killed him in self-defense, or he would have killed me.\" [2477] \"You said he didn't explicitly say he would kill you.\" [2478] \"No, but I knew he would. What do you think, Mr. Cattermole? Did I kill Richard Nigg?\" [2479] \"I don't know.\" [2480] \"Cattermole, my friend, don't be silly.\" [2481] \"He's not silly.\" Jinnie said. \"He's using the part of his brain you don't want to use, Mr. Poirot. Please, just think again, please. I would like to hear you say I didn't murder Richard Nigg before I'm hanged.\" [2482] I stood up and said: \"Let's go, Poirot. I wanted to end this conversation while the echo of 'hope' still lingered in the room.\" [2483] Epilogue\n[2484] Four days later, I sat by the fireplace in Mrs. Blanche Onsworth's house, sipping a glass of brandy and engrossed in a crossword puzzle when Poirot entered the living room and stood silently beside me for several minutes. I didn't look up at him. [2485] Finally, he cleared his throat and said: \"Caderpillar, you still won't talk to me about how Richard Nigg died - whether he was murdered, committed suicide with assistance, or killed in self-defense?\" [2486] \"I don't see the point in arguing about it,\" I said, feeling a spasm in my stomach. I no longer wanted to discuss the murder at the Baskerville Hotel. What I wanted to do, or needed to do, was to write it all down, to put the whole story on paper in detail. Was there such a difference between saying something and writing it down? [2487] \"Don't be afraid, my friend,\" said Poirot. \"I won't mention it again. We can talk about something else, like how I went to the 'Jolly Coffee Shop' this morning. Miss Spence asked me to tell you that she'd like to talk to you when you're free. She's a bit upset.\" [2488] \"With me?\" [2489] \"Yes. She said she was just hearing about the case at the Baskerville Hotel one moment, and the next, it was all over. A murder happened right in front of everyone, and yet no one saw the end. Miss Spence wants you to tell her the whole story from beginning to end.\" [2490] \"It wasn't my fault that murder happened,\" I whispered. \"Why can't she read about it in the newspaper like everyone else?\" [2491] \"No, she wants to talk to you alone. As a waitress, she's quite intelligent and a rare woman. Don't you think so, my friend?\" [2492] \"I know what you're up to, Poirot,\" I said weakly. \"Just drop it, stop wasting time, and tell Phyllida... sorry... you see, I'm busy. Can you leave?\" [2493] \"You're angry with me.\" [2494] \"Yes, a bit,\" I admitted. \"Henry Nayes and the suitcase, Raffles Burbank and the laundry cart, Thomas Brine and his girlfriend in the hotel garden, who was wearing a light brown coat, half of England wears such coats. And the handcart\u2026\"\n[2495] \"Ah!\" [2496] \"Yes, 'Ah'. You know that Jeanne Hobby isn't dead, yet you deliberately mislead me, making me suspect that her body was moved from Room 402, in the most impractical ways?\" [2497] \"My friend, because I wanted to encourage you to use your imagination. If you don't consider the most unlikely possibilities, you can't become the best detective. I'm teaching you to use your grey cells, forcing them to move in a different direction, so that you can be inspired.\" [2498] \"Do as you please.\" [2499] \"You must think that Poirot is going too far, beyond what is necessary. Maybe so.\" [2500] \"In Room 402, you made a fuss over those bloodstains, the direction of the bloodstains, the width of the door... What does it all mean? You know that Jeanne Hobby wasn't killed and wasn't dragged anywhere.\" [2501] \"I know, but you don't. You and our friend Mr. Lazari both believe that Jeanie is dead and that the blood on the floor belongs to her. Oh, I want you to think for yourself: a suitcase, a wheeled laundry cart - these two tools can directly enter Room 402 and be placed next to the body. So why did the killer drag the body towards the door? He couldn't have done that! Therefore, she couldn't have been dead! The bloodstains dragged towards the door were a deception, intended to make us believe that the body was moved elsewhere. This detail was done very convincingly, so it was very important as evidence at the crime scene. [2502] \"But for Hercule Poirot, it was just a small detail that allowed him to verify his suspicions. Jeanie Hobbs was not killed in that room, and no one was killed. Because I can't think of a reason to drag a bloody body to the door. No killer would walk around the hotel's public corridor with a victim's body, unless they put it in a container. But I can think of containers that can easily be brought into the room and directly put the body in and take it away, without dragging the body to the door. Caderpillar, this logic is very simple. I'm surprised you didn't think of it immediately.\" [2503] \"It's simple for you.\" I said, \"Next time, if you want me to think of something immediately, just tell me directly. Whatever it is, be direct, and it will save a lot of trouble.\" [2504] He smiled. \"Okay. I will learn to be more direct from my good friend Caderpillar. I will start learning now!\" [2505] \"If by 'private affairs' you mean Phyllis Spoon, I can tell you that we are impossible, forever impossible.\" I glanced at the letter in his hand. \"What about the private affairs of another pitiful person? Why thank you?\" [2506] Poirot smiled. \"Dr. Ambrose Flavard and his wife.\u201d He handed me the letter. [2507] \"Who came to the letter?\u201d\n[2508] Poirot smiled. \"Ambrose Flavard, M.D., and his wife.\u201d He handed me the letter.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nGleeholm has at least one advantage over London \u2013 they know how to ignore a person and make them feel uncomfortable.\" [1106] \"I hope you make a serious promise, Mr. Cuthbertson.\" [1107] \"Why the secrecy? Isn't everyone in the village aware of the Yew couple and what happened to them?\" [1108] Margaret's next words tell me she is concerned about one person in the village. She says, \"After hearing what I have to say, you will surely want to find Ambrose Flavard, the doctor.\" [1109] \"Why keep mentioning him if you want me to forget about him?\" [1110] Her face flushed. \"You must promise not to find him, and if you happen to encounter him, you cannot mention Patrick and Francis Yew. Unless you promise me, I will not tell you anything.\" [1111] \"I'm afraid I cannot promise that. What am I supposed to report back to the Scotland Yard leaders? They sent me here to investigate the situation.\" [1112] \"Well, we're at a dead end.\" Margaret crossed her arms. [1113] \"What if I go to him and ask him to tell me the stories? He knows Mr. and Mrs. Yves, right? You said yesterday that he lived in Glebeholme when they were alive.\" [1114] \"No!\" Her eyes filled with fear. \"Please don't find Ambrose! You don't understand. You won't understand.\" [1115] \"What are you afraid of, Margaret? In my opinion, you're an honest person, but... well, now I can't help but wonder if you're hiding something from me.\" [1116] \"Oh, I'll tell you everything, with no reservations.\" [1117] For some reason, I trusted her. \"So, if you're not holding anything back, why won't you let me talk to anyone else about the Evanses?\" [1118] Margaret stood up and walked to the window, pressing her forehead against the glass, her entire body blocking my view of the Evanses' grave. \"The incident that happened in 1913 left a painful wound on this village,\" she said calmly. \"Everyone who lives here couldn't escape it. After that, Nancy Duane moved to London, Richard Nigg moved to Devonshire, but they couldn't escape either, carrying their wounds with them. They may not show it on their skin or bodies, but the wounds are there. The invisible wounds are the most painful. As for those who stayed, like Ambrose Flavard, they are also suffering. I don't know if Gleeholm can recover, but I know it hasn't yet.\" [1119] She turned her head to look at me. \"Mr. Kitcher, no one in the village has mentioned the tragedy since then. No one, not directly. Sometimes silence is the only way. Silence and forgetting, if only someone could forget.\" [1120] \"Are you worried that my questions might affect Dr. Flavard? Is he trying to forget the past?\" [1121] \"As I said, forgetting is impossible.\" [1122] \"But... is it a painful topic for him?\" [1123] \"Yes, it's very painful.\" [1124] \"Is he your friend?\" [1125] \"I have nothing to do with this,\" she rebuked me sternly. \"Ambrose is a good person, and I don't want him to be disturbed. Why won't you swear?\" [1126] \"Okay, I swear.\" I reluctantly promised her, \"I won't mention anything you said to anyone in the village.\" [1127] The three portraits of the late Charles Ernest on the wall were staring at me, and his eyes seemed to be warning me: \"You scoundrel, if you dare to deceive my wife, you will regret it.\" [1128] \"Is your heart at peace?\" I asked her, \"You don't want me to go to Dr. Flavard and talk about it because you're afraid he'll be sad. But I'm worried that my words may make you sad. I don't want you to be sad.\" [1129] \"Well.\" Margaret sighed, \"In fact, I also want to take this opportunity to tell this story to someone like me.\" [1130] \"Then, please start.\" I said. [1131] She nods and sits back in her chair, beginning to tell the story of Patrick and Francis Ives, without interruption from me. Here is the story as I write it down now:\n[1132] All the trouble started sixteen years ago, from a rumor spread by a maid who worked for the Reverend Patrick Ives and his wife, Francis. Although the rumor started with her, she was not responsible for the later tragedies, nor did she bear the main responsibility. She spun a vicious lie, but only told one person, and had no part in the process of spreading it throughout the village. Indeed, after the unpleasant incident, she withdrew almost all of what she had said before, and no one saw her again. Some people speculate that she was ashamed of what she had done, so she hid herself away, and she should have been ashamed. Later, she regretted what she had done and tried to make amends, but it was too late. [1133] Of course, telling one person and spreading such a lie was immoral. Perhaps it was because she had a difficult day, or perhaps, as a maid, she naturally resented the Ives couple. Perhaps she wanted to spice up her dull life with a little malicious lie and thought it wouldn't hurt anyone else seriously. Unfortunately, she chose Harriet Sipper as her only listener for her vile lie. She may have chosen her for a very simple reason: since her husband's death, Harriet had become bitter and resentful, and hearing such a lie would excite her and she would believe it, as she hoped it was true. There was someone in the village who had made a serious mistake, and even worse (in Harriet's eyes, this would be even better), it was the clergyman! She would certainly be delighted to hear the news and shine with excitement! Yes, Harriet was the best listener for the maid's vile lie, and she rightly chose her. [1134] The maid told Harriet Sipper that Patrick Ives was a cruel liar who deserved to be damned. She said that while his wife Francis was out helping the parishioners, Patrick seduced the villagers late at night, claiming he could help them communicate with their deceased loved ones through spiritualism, claiming that deceased souls had entrusted him to convey their messages, and thus defrauding them of their money. [1135] The end. [1136] Harriet Sipper spread the rumor that Patrick had already swindled several villagers with this trick. She may have done this to escalate the situation and make the story more terrifying. Later, the maid insisted that she had only mentioned one name to Harriet, that of Nancy Duane. [1137] At that time, Nancy was not yet a famous portrait painter, but an ordinary woman. In 1910, her husband William took up the position of headmaster at the Grellhollow village school, and she came with him. William was much older than Nancy, and when they married, Nancy was eighteen, and William was almost fifty. He died of respiratory disease in 1912. [1138] In the malicious rumor spread by Harriet Sipper in the snowy January of 1913, Nancy had made several late-night or evening visits to the vicar's house, each time when it was already dark. Nancy always seemed shifty and chose the days when Frances Eve was not at home. [1139] A sensible person would not believe such a story. In the dark, you can't tell if someone has a shifty expression on their face, and in fact, you can't see any expression at all. In the dark, you can't tell who the woman entering or leaving the vicar's house is, unless her gait is very distinctive, and Nancy Duane's gait was not particularly special. Of course, it is more likely that someone saw her several times and followed her home to find out who she was. [1140] It is much easier to accept the views of someone more fanatical than to challenge them, and that is what the people of Grellhollow did. They willingly believed the rumor and began to condemn Patrick Eve along with Harriet, condemning him for profaning the gods and extorting money. Most people believed (or pretended to believe in order to avoid being slandered by Harriet) that Patrick Eve had deceived the parishioners by claiming to give messages to the living and the dead, charging exorbitant fees. In the eyes of the villagers of Grellhollow, Nancy Duane would not have been able to refuse such an arrangement, especially as it was provided by the parish vicar. Of course, she would have paid a considerable sum for such an arrangement. [1141] The villagers forgot that they knew, liked, and trusted Patrick Ives. They ignored the fact that he was a decent and kind man, knowing that Harriet Cyples was fond of digging up dirt on bad people, but they did not care. They agreed with Harriet Cyples because they did not want to anger her, but this was not the only reason. The two allies of Harriet Cyples also played an important role: Richard Nix and Ada Granceberry supported her cause. [1142] It is well known that Ada is the most devout woman in Gregholm. Her faith never wavered, and she always quoted from the New Testament when she spoke. She was admired and respected by everyone, but she was not a companion you would seek out when you had time to spare. She had once been engaged to the acknowledged young scholar Richard Nix, the lawyer. [1143] Richard's exceptional intellect and noble demeanor earned him the respect of the entire village. He believed Harriet's lies because he had witnessed them firsthand. He had seen Nancy Duane, or a woman who looked like Nancy Duane, enter and leave the vicar's house on more than one occasion, and the vicar's wife was out visiting her father or attending to the needs of the parishioners. [1144] Richard Nix believed the rumors, and Ada Granceberry believed them too. She could not bear the thought of a clergyman like Patrick engaging in such pagan behavior. She, Harriet, and Richard decided to remove Patrick Ives from his position as the parish vicar of Gregholm and expel him from the church. They wanted Patrick to confess his sins in public, but he refused to do so, believing the rumors to be unfounded. [1145] The villagers' hatred of Patrick Ives soon spread to his wife, Francis, who they believed must have known about her husband's deception. Francis swore she did not know. At first, she tried to insist that Patrick would not do such a thing, but people kept saying it, and she eventually fell silent. [1146] In Gleeholm, only two people refused to participate in the persecution of the Eve couple: Nancy Duane and Ambrose Flavord, the doctor who had publicly defended Francis Eve. He argued that if Francis knew such vile things were happening in her own home, why wasn't she present every time? Couldn't that prove her innocence? Ambrose Flavord also pointed out that it was impossible to discern guilt from someone's facial expression in the dark. He publicly stated that he would always believe his friend Patrick Eve, unless someone could provide undeniable evidence of the clergyman's wrongdoing. Ambrose Flavord also accused Harriet Sipper (in public, in front of everyone) of planting more dirt on Patrick Eve in half an hour than all the bad things he had done in his lifetime. [1147] Despite this, Ambrose Flavord's viewpoint was not widely accepted, but he was one of the few who didn't care what others thought. He defended Patrick Eve in front of the church authorities, telling them that he believed the rumors to be baseless. He was also deeply concerned about Francis Eve's well-being, as she was in a terrible state. She stopped eating and barely slept, and would collapse whenever she had to leave the parsonage for even a moment. Patrick Eve was also mad, saying that his position as a clergyman and his reputation no longer mattered to him; all he wanted was for his wife to recover. [1148] Meanwhile, Nancy Duane said nothing, neither confirming nor denying the rumors. The more Harriet provoked her, the more she seemed to remain silent. One day, Harriet suddenly changed her mind. She found Victor Mickle, telling him there was something important he needed to know that could stop this madness from continuing. After hearing her out, Victor Mickle chuckled to himself, rubbing his hands together, and slipped out the back door of the inn. Soon, everyone in Gleeholm knew that Nancy Duane had something to announce. [1149] Besides the Eve couple, the entire village went to the King's Head Inn, including the maid who had first stirred up trouble, who had been missing for several weeks and now rushed over, eager to see how the drama would unfold. [1150] Nancy Duane smiled at Ambrose Flavord before calmly telling everyone that Patrick Eve's claim of receiving payment for helping her communicate with her deceased husband was false, but she admitted that the rumors were not entirely untrue. She confessed that she had indeed visited the parsonage multiple times when the parson's wife was not present, as she had fallen in love with Patrick Eve. [1151] The whole village was stunned. Some began to whisper, some covered their mouths with their hands, and others tightly grasped the arms of the people next to them. [1152] After the commotion subsided, Nancy Duane continued: \"Our secret meetings and mutual seduction were wrong, but we couldn't help it. Every time we met at his house, we just talked about our feelings for each other and about how we couldn't be together. We both thought we shouldn't meet again, but as soon as Francis went out... Oh, the power of love is irresistible.\" [1153] Suddenly, someone shouted, \"You only talked? What nonsense!\" Nancy once again assured everyone that she and Patrick Yves had no physical contact. [1154] \"I have told you the truth,\" Nancy continued, \"I didn't want to say the truth, but it's the only way to stop the stupid rumors from spreading. If someone knows what it's like to be irresistibly in love, they won't blame me and Patrick. And those who secretly condemn us in their hearts, you don't understand love, and I pity you.\" [1155] Then, Nancy stared straight at Harriet Sipper and said, \"Harriet, I believe you once understood the meaning of true love, but after losing George, you chose to forget everything. Even turned love into hate.\" [1156] At this point, Harriet Sipper stood up, determined to prove herself innocent. She not only immediately condemned Nancy as a lying whore but also condemned Patrick Ivory even more madly: accusing him of not only making money through fraud in the name of spiritualism but also of being unfaithful to his wife by consorting with morally corrupt women. She called Patrick a pagan, an adulterer! More evil than she had ever imagined! She also said that a sinful person like him had no right to call himself a pastor of Gleghorn, which was a shame. [1157] Nancy Duane couldn't bear Harriet's fierce attack and left the White Hart Inn in the middle. A few seconds later, the maid from the Ivory family also ran out of the inn, red-faced and crying. [1158] Most of the villagers were at a loss and confused. At this point, Ada Gransby stood up to support Harriet. She said that although she didn't know which were facts and which were rumors, there was one thing that was certain: Patrick Ivory had to be guilty, so he couldn't be the pastor of Gleghorn anymore. [1159] Yes, most of the villagers agreed. Yes, that's right. [1160] At that time, Richard Nigges said nothing, even when his fianc\u00e9e Ada pointed to him to say something, he didn't say anything. Later that day, he told Ambrose Flavard, the doctor, that the sudden turn of events worried him. He said, \"The phrase 'a sinner' clearly pleased Ada, but he couldn't accept it himself. He also said that Harriet Sipper's accusation of Patrick Ivory's two sins together disgusted him. In the absence of evidence and without a chance to defend himself, she twisted Nancy Duane's \"it's not like that, it's like that\" into \"it's like that, and it's like that.\" [1161] Ada used the word \"without a doubt\" at the King's Head Inn. Richard Nixes told Ambrose Flavard that now, in his opinion, it was without a doubt that people (including himself, which made him feel ashamed) were slandering Patrick Ives. What if Nancy Duane was lying? What if her love was just unrequited, and Patrick Ives agreed to meet her secretly just to explain that she should keep her feelings to herself? [1162] Dr. Flavard also agreed: no one could be sure whether Patrick Ives had done something wrong or not, and it was from the beginning that others had arbitrarily defined it. As the only one who was willing to let him into their house, the doctor went to visit him again and told Patrick everything Nancy Duane had said at the King's Head Inn. Patrick only shook his head after hearing it, and did not make any comments on whether what Nancy said was true or false. As for Frances Ives, both physically and mentally, she was getting worse and worse. [1163] Richard Nixes failed to convince Ada Gransbury to accept his point of view, so their relationship became tense. Under Harriet's leadership, the villagers continued to condemn the Ives couple, shouting at them day and night outside the vicarage. At the same time, Ada also petitioned the church to expel the Ives couple from the vicarage and the Glynhill parish. [1164] Tragedy then occurred. Frances Ives could no longer bear these insults and committed suicide by poisoning, ending her miserable life. When Patrick found her, he knew it was too late, and there was no need to call Dr. Flavard. Frances could not be saved. Patrick knew that he could no longer survive in the guilt and suffering, so he also ended his life. [1165] Ada Gransbury suggested that the villagers pray for the souls of Patrick and Frances Ives, asking God to forgive them, although it was unlikely that God would forgive them. [1166] Harriet Sipper believed that there was no need for God to judge this matter, and that the Eves couple would be forever tormented by the fires of hell. She told the \"executors of justice\" who followed her that the Eves couple deserved it. [1167] After the death of the Eves couple, Richard Niggles broke off his engagement with Edith Gransbury and left Gleholling within a few months. Nancy Duane went to London, and the maid who had spun the terrible lies disappeared without a trace, and the villagers never saw her again. [1168] At the same time, Charles and Margaret Ernst came to the village and took over the parish.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nOtherwise, why did they plan to have dinner together and then go their separate ways?\" [517] \"I don't believe a woman who is angry would carry a cup of half-drunk tea away,\" Poirot said. \"By the time she got back to Room 121, the tea would have cooled.\" [518] \"I often drink cold tea,\" I said. \"I like it.\" [519] Poirot raised his eyebrows. \"If I didn't know you were honest, I wouldn't believe what you just said. Cold tea! A lie!\" [520] \"Oh, I should say I'm gradually starting to like cold tea,\" I defended myself. \"I don't have to rush when drinking it. I can drink it whenever I want, and it won't spoil. I value that lack of time constraints and pressure.\" [521] There was a knock on the door. \"It's probably Lazari, to see if anyone's disturbing our important conversation,\" I said. [522] \"Come in,\" Poirot said loudly. [523] It wasn't Lazari, but Thomas Brine, the young employee who had seen Richard Nigg in the elevator at 7:30. \"Ah, Mr. Brine,\" Poirot said. \"Come in. The information you provided last night was very helpful to us, Mr. Cader and I are both very grateful to you.\" [524] \"Yes, very grateful,\" I said sincerely. I had done my best to make him relax and get everything off his chest, but it was clear he still had something on his mind. The poor guy looked just as tense as he had in the restaurant. His hands were wringing and his forehead was covered in sweat, and he looked even paler than before. [525] \"I've let you down,\" he said. \"I've let Lazari down, too. He's always been so good to me, so good.\" He stopped, wringing his hands even more vigorously. [526] \"You didn't tell us the truth?\" Polo asked. [527] \"Every word I said was true, sir!\" Thomas Brine said angrily. \"If I lied to the police about such an important matter, it would be just like that murderer!\" [528] \"I don't think you're as bad as him, sir.\" [529] \"There are two things I forgot to say. I'm really sorry, sir. You see, speaking in front of so many people is not easy for me, and I never dare to speak in front of many people. And the situation just now was even worse... \" He nodded in the direction of the restaurant. \"I don't want to say this because Mr. Nigges praised me.\" [530] \"Praised?\" [531] \"I understand, I didn't do anything worth praising, sir. I'm just an ordinary employee, not an important person. I do my job, earn my salary, and do my best, but I'm not good enough to be singled out for praise.\" [532] \"But Mr. Nigges praised you, didn't he?\" Poirot asked, \"He singled you out?\" [533] Brine hesitated. \"Yes, sir. As I said earlier, I didn't ask him to do that, I didn't do anything worth praising. But at the time, I saw him, and he saw me. He said to me, 'Ah, Mr. Brine, you work very efficiently, and I trust you.' After that, he talked about the bill, and he wanted it to be under his name.\" [534] \"You didn't want to repeat in front of everyone that he praised you, right? You were afraid people would say you were bragging?\" I asked. [535] \"Yes, sir, that's exactly it. But there's one more thing. After we settled the bill, Mr. Nigges asked me to get him a glass of sherry. It was me who gave it to him. I said I'd bring it to his room later, but he said he'd wait there. So I poured it and gave it to him, and he took it and went to the elevator.\" [536] Poirot leaned forward. \"And yet, when I asked in the restaurant if anyone had given Richard Nigges a sherry, you didn't say anything?\" [537] Brierley looked bewildered and disappointed, as if the right answer was on the tip of his tongue, but he didn't know why it disappeared. \"I should have said it, sir. I should have told you and the three victims when you asked. I regret not fulfilling my duty. May God rest their souls! I hope that by telling you now, I can make up for my mistake.\" [538] \"Yes, yes. But, sir, I'm curious, why didn't you say it in the restaurant? When I asked, 'Has anyone given Richard Nigges a sherry?' what stopped you from admitting it?\" [539] The poor waiter started shivering again. \"Mr. Poirot, I swear by my late mother's name that I have told you everything that happened between me and Mr. Nigges yesterday. All the details. Please rest assured, that's everything that happened yesterday, with no omissions.\" [540] Poirot opened his mouth to ask another question, but I intervened. \"Mr. Brierley, thank you very much. You don't need to feel bad about not telling us sooner. I understand how difficult it is to stand up in front of so many people and say something. I don't like doing it myself.\" [541] As soon as he said he could leave, Thomas Burrnell darted out the door like a fox that had just escaped a hound. [542] \"I believe him,\" I said to Poirot when he was gone. \"He told us everything he knows.\" [543] \"Yes, he was truthful about meeting Richard Nehy by the hotel elevator. But he omitted details about himself. Why didn't he mention the sherry in the dining room? I asked him twice, and he didn't answer. He only confessed and apologized sincerely. He didn't lie, but he also didn't stand up to clear his name. Ah, how he could have hidden it! That's lying too, even worse, he doesn't have to worry about his lie being exposed!\" [544] Poirot suddenly laughed. \"And you, Kitts, trying to protect him from me, Hercule Poirot, repeating the same thing over and over again. Is it because you don't want me to get any information, ah?\" [545] \"He looked like he was about to collapse. And to be honest, if he's hiding something, it must be something that he thinks is irrelevant to us but important to him. He's a fragile and cautious man, and his sense of responsibility would force him to tell us if he thinks it's crucial.\" [546] \"But you let him go. I didn't have a chance to tell him that the information he withheld might be crucial.\" Poirot raised his voice, looking at me with a glare to show his anger, \"Even I, Hercule Poirot, don't know what's related and what's not. So I have to find out everything. He stood up suddenly and said, \"I'm going to 'The Joyful Cafe' now. The coffee there is much better than Mr. Lazarus's.\" [547] \"But Richard Nigges' brother Henry is on his way. I thought you wanted to talk to him.\" [548] \"I need a change of scenery, Cader. I have to activate my gray brain cells, or they'll go on strike if I don't take them somewhere else.\" [549] \"Nonsense! You just want to see Jean or get news about her.\" I objected to him leaving. [550] \"Maybe so. But if there's a killer out there who's targeting geese, what would you do? Bring Mr. Henry Nigges to 'The Joyful Cafe' and talk to him there.\" [551] \"What? He's traveled all the way from Devonshire and probably doesn't want to go out again right after arriving at the hotel\u2014\"\n[552] \"Does he want a dead goose?\" Poirot commanded, \"Just ask him.\" [553] I didn't want to ask Henry Nigus such a question, and I was worried that he would turn around and leave, thinking that the head of Scotland Yard was crazy. [554] Chapter Seven\n[555] Two Keys\n[556] When Poirot arrived at \"The Happy Coffee Shop,\" he found it very busy, with a mix of cigarette smoke and pancake syrup in the air. \"I want a table,\" he complained to Phyllida Spence, who was standing by the wooden coat rack, still wearing her coat. As she took off her hat, her tangled hair crackled with static electricity, standing on end for several seconds before slowly falling back into place. Poirot found the effect amusing. [557] \"It's not easy to accommodate you, is it?\" she said cheerfully. \"Even if you're a great detective, I can't kick out customers to make room for you. But I'll tell you what, the 'Oh, Cecil' couple should have left by now. Wait a bit, and you can sit at their table.\" [558] \"Oh, Cecil? What an odd name.\" [559] Phyllida smiled and whispered, \"Oh, Cecil' is the wife's catchphrase. She's always saying it. The poor man doesn't even get a word in edgeways before she starts bossing him around. If he says he wants scrambled eggs and toast, she'll say, 'Oh, Cecil, don't have scrambled eggs and toast!' I don't think he dares to argue with her! He sat down at the first table as soon as he came in, and she immediately said, 'Oh, Cecil, don't sit here.' It's obvious that he should have said the opposite, wanted what he didn't want, and didn't want what he wanted. That's what I would do. I've been waiting to see him stand up to her. But honestly, I think he's a useless old thing, with a brain like a moldy cabbage, no wonder she's always saying 'Oh, Cecil.'\" [560] \"If he doesn't hurry up and leave, I'll go over and say 'Oh, Cecil',\" Poirot said, standing with legs aching and desperately wanting to find a seat. [561] \"They'll be gone before your coffee arrives,\" Phyllida said. \"You see, she's finished her meal and will soon say 'Oh, Osceola' to get him to leave. Why did you come for lunch? Oh, wait, let me guess. You're here to see Jenny, right? And I heard you were here early this morning.\" [562] \"How did you know that?\" Poirot asked. \"You're here now, too.\" [563] \"I never left,\" Phyllida said mysteriously. \"Nobody knows where she is, not even a hair. But I've been thinking about her, too, just like you, Poirot.\" [564] \"You're worried about her, too?\" Poirot asked. [565] \"Oh, I'm not worried about her being in danger, it's not my job to save her,\" Phyllida said. [566] \"Yes, it's not yours either.\" [567] \"It's not yours either.\" [568] \"Ah, but Hercule Poirot is a savior, he has saved many innocent people from death.\" [569] \"Maybe half of them were guilty,\" said Phyllida cheerfully, as if the idea was amusing. [570] \"Oh, lady, you're a bit cynical.\" [571] \"You could say that. I only know that if I worried about every sad person who comes here, I would never have a moment's peace. Life is one problem after another, but most of them are imagined, not real issues.\" [572] \"What if the imagined ones become real?\" Poirot said. [573] \"Baseless imaginations won't become real, most of them don't. As for Jean, last night I did find something... but I can't remember. I remember thinking, 'Jean did that, how funny,' or 'she said that.' The problem is, I can't remember what she did or said that made me think that. I tried so hard to remember, but my head was spinning. Ah, look, 'Oh, the Cecil' couple is leaving. You should sit down. Coffee?\" [574] \"Sure, thank you. Madam, can you think of anything Jean did or said? It's more important to me than anything!\" [575] \"More important than a straight-backed chair? Or arranging the cutlery neatly on the table?\" Fay suddenly said sarcastically. [576] \"Oh, you think those worries are imagined, do you?\" Poirot asked. [577] Phryne blushed. \"If I offended you, I'm sorry,\" she said, \"but... er, if you didn't worry so much about the forks not being perfectly aligned, you'd be happier, wouldn't you?\" [578] Poirot made his best friendly smile. \"If you can remember something Jean did that made a deep impression on you, I'd be happier.\u201d He strode confidently to the table and sat down. [579] Poirot waited at the \"Jolly Coffee Shop\" for an hour and a half, during which he enjoyed a delicious lunch, but still didn't see Jean. [580] Around 2 pm, I led a man into the \"Jolly Coffee Shop.\" Poirot initially mistook him for Henry Nigel, Richard's brother. The situation was a bit confusing, and I explained that I had instructed Stanley Bill Detective Inspector to bring Nigel here as soon as he arrived. The only person I was concerned about now was the one standing next to me. [581] I introduced him as Samuel Kid, a boiler maker. Poirot recoiled at Kid's dirty, unbuttoned shirt and half-shaven face, and I couldn't help but chuckle. Kid's beard was neither a full beard nor a goatee, but rather a result of his ineptitude with a razor. His face bore the marks of a failed attempt at shaving, so he had stopped midway. As a result, one side of his face was smooth and beard-free, but with scratches, while the other side was covered in dark, unkempt hair, making it difficult to determine which side looked worse. \"Mr. Kid has a story to tell that will interest you,\" I said. \"While waiting for Henry Nigel at the Bluxton Hotel, I suddenly\u2014\"\n[582] \"Ah!\" Poirot interrupted. \"You and Mr. Kid came from the Bluxton Hotel?\" [583] \"Yes,\" I thought he was from Timbuktu. [584] \"How did you get here?\" [585] \"Lazari arranged for a hotel car to pick us up.\" [586] \"How long did it take?\" [587] \"Exactly thirty minutes.\" [588] \"How was the traffic? Were there many cars?\" [589] \"Not really. You could say there were almost none.\" [590] \"Do you think it's possible to get here in less time?\" Poirot asked. [591] \"Unless I grew wings. I think thirty minutes is already very fast.\" [592] \"Okay. Mr. Kid, please sit down and tell Poirot your interesting story.\" [593] Surprisingly, Samuel Kid did not sit down, but instead laughed loudly, repeating Poirot's words with an exaggerated French or Belgian accent. \"Mr. Kid, please sit down and tell the bellows your interesting story.\" [594] Poirot was insulted by the mocking of his pronunciation. I suddenly felt sympathy for him until he said, \"Mr. Kid, your pronunciation of my name is better than yours, Mr. Kakirpur.\" [595] \"Mr. Kid,\" the half-shaven man laughed, \"Oh, sir, please don't mind, I was just entertaining myself. Mr. Kid!\" [596] \"We're not here for amusement,\" I was getting impatient with his antics, so I told him, \"Repeat what you said to me outside the hotel.\" [597] Mr. Kid took ten minutes to tell a story that could be summarized in a few sentences, but it was valuable. Last night, just after eight o'clock, Mr. Kid was passing by the Brooks Han Hotel when he saw a woman come out of the door and run down the steps, heading straight for the street. She was out of breath and looked terrified. He wanted to ask her if she needed help, but she ran too fast, and he couldn't catch up. She dropped something on the ground as she ran, and it was two golden keys. When she realized she had dropped them, she rushed back to pick them up. She was wearing gloves and holding the two golden keys tightly, disappearing into the night. [598] \"I even thought to myself, how strange, why was she in such a hurry,\" said Samuel Kid thoughtfully. \"This morning, I saw the police everywhere, so I asked what had happened. When I heard that there had been several murders, I thought to myself, 'Sammy, the person you saw last night may very well be the killer.' She looked terrified, didn't she? Terrified!\" [599] Poirot stared intently at a stain on Mr. Kid's dirty shirt and murmured, \"Terrified. Your story is very interesting, Mr. Kid. You said there were two keys?\" [600] \"Yes, sir, two golden keys.\" [601] \"Were you close enough to see them clearly?\" [602] \"Yes, sir. The streetlights outside the Brooks Han Hotel were bright, and I could see them clearly.\" [603] \"What else can you tell me about those keys, besides them being golden?\" [604] \"They had numbers on them.\" [605] \"Numbers?\" I said. Samuel Kid hadn't mentioned this detail when he first told me the story outside the hotel, and he didn't mention it again when he recounted it on the way to the caf\u00e9. Darn it, I should have asked him. I had seen Richard Nigges' key, the one Hercule Poirot found behind a loose fireplace brick, which had the number \"238\" on it. [606] \"Yes, sir, they have numbers. You know, hundreds and thousands like that...\"\n[607] \"I know what numbers are,\" I interrupted impatiently. [608] \"Are these the numbers on the keys, Mr. Kid?\" Polo asked. \"Hundreds and thousands?\" [609] \"No, sir. If I'm not mistaken, one of them is a hundred and some change. The other is\u2026\"\n[610] \"I remember it being around three hundred and something\u2026 of course, I can't be sure, but that's what I saw. One was around a hundred and something, and the other was around three hundred and something.\" Harryte Sipper lives at 121, and Edith Gransberry lives at 317. [611] Suddenly, I felt empty inside, and I knew what it meant. When I first saw the three corpses and heard the forensic expert say that each of them had a gold cufflink with a letter combination in their mouths, that's what I felt. [612] It now appears that Samuel Kid almost bumped into the murderer last night, a woman who looked terrified. I shuddered. [613] \"The woman you saw, does she have light blonde hair, a brown hat, and a brown coat?\" Poirot asked. [614] Clearly, he was thinking of Jenny again, but I remained convinced that Jenny had nothing to do with it. However, I understood Poirot's reasoning: Jenny had roamed through the whole city of London last night, agitated, and so had this woman.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHercule Poirot rarely makes mistakes, but occasionally they happen, and this must be the case, because room 238 was indeed very tidy, without a trace of disorder, just as tidy as Harriet Sipper and Ada Gransberry's rooms. [329] \"I have to close the window and see if there's anything different.\" Poirot muttered to himself. After closing the window, he re-examined the room, still feeling uneasy. He just didn't like room 238, even if he came to the Brouks Hotel and went straight to this room, he would still feel uncomfortable. [330] Suddenly, a question popped into his mind, interrupting his thoughts. The fireplace! There was a tile that didn't align properly on the fireplace, it was uneven and protruded a little, a bit loose. There was a loose tile in the room, and Poirot wouldn't be able to sleep. Poirot stared at Richard Nigel's corpse and said to it, \"If I were in this position, no, there's no if. [331] As soon as Poirot touched it, the tile came off with a thud, and something else fell with it: a key with \"238\" written on it. \"My God,\" Poirot muttered, \"it seems the search was not thorough enough.\" [332] Poirot put the key back in place and began to examine other parts of the room, inch by inch, but found nothing. He then went to room 317 and finally room 121. I finished my task and was preparing to report the exciting new situation to him when he was still there. [333] Poirot was still the same, insisting on saying his discovery first, how he discovered the key. I can only say that in Belgium, he did not seem too arrogant. He said proudly, \"Understand my meaning, my friend? The window was not opened by Richard Nigel, but was opened after his death! The killer locked the door of room 238 and needed to find a way to escape. So he hid the key behind a loose tile and then climbed down outside the window. It's also possible that he loosened that tile. [334] \"Why didn't the killer carry the key with him and leave the room in a normal way?\" I asked. [335] \"Why didn't the killer carry the key with him and leave the room in a normal way?\" I asked. [336] \"I've been asking myself that question, but so far, I haven't found an answer,\" Poirot said. \"I have found out that there are no keys hidden in this room or Room 317. The murderer must have taken the two keys with him when he left the Blaukopf Hotel, but why didn't he take the third one? Why did he treat Richard Nigges differently?\" [337] \"I don't have any good ideas,\" I said. \"Listen, I've talked to John Good, the employee -\"\n[338] \"The most reliable employee,\" Poirot corrected me with a wink. [339] \"Yes, indeed. Well... whether he's reliable or not, the information he provided has been very helpful. You're right, the three victims were related. I found the addresses of the deceased: Harriet Sipper and Ada Gransberry both lived in a place called Gleeholm in the Pigeon Valley.\" [340] \"Good. What about Richard Nigges?\" [341] \"He lives in Bideford, Devon. But he's connected too; he booked all three rooms\u2014Edith's, Harriet's, and his own, and paid the deposits in advance.\" [342] \"Really? That's interesting\u2026\" Poirot muttered, stroking his mustache. [343] \"My feeling is very strange,\" I said. \"The biggest mystery is: if Harriet Westpall and Edith Gransbury were supposed to leave from the same village on the same day, why didn't they come together? Why didn't they come to the hotel together? I asked John Good about this several times, and he insisted that Harriet arrived two hours before Edith on Wednesday, exactly two hours.\" [344] \"What about Richard Neville?\" [345] I decided to clarify any information related to Neville first so that I wouldn't have to keep answering Poirot's \"What about Richard Neville?\" questions. [346] \"He arrived an hour earlier than Harriet Sipper, the earliest of the three, but it was not John Good who received him. It was a young employee, Thomas Brineau. I also found out that all three victims came to London by train, none of them drove. Do you want to know these details, but \u2014\"\n[347] \"I need to know everything,\" Poirot said. [348] It was clear that he wanted to take charge and investigate the case himself, which both annoyed and reassured me. \"Braunsan Hotel has several cars that go to the station to pick people up,\" I told Poirot. \"The fees are high, but they are willing to wait for you. Three weeks ago, Richard Nigges booked three cars through John Good to pick him up, Harriet Sipper, and Ada Gransbury separately. And all the fees, rooms, and car rentals were paid in advance by Nigges.\" [349] \"I want to know if he is wealthy,\" Poirot said thoughtfully. \"There are too many cases of people being killed for money. What do you think, Captain Calver, do we have more clues now?\" [350] \"Hmm...,\" I said, deciding to be more proactive since he asked. In Poirot's dictionary, imagining the possibilities of the case was a good thing, so I decided to summarize a theory based on the facts. \"Richard Nigges booked the rooms and paid the rent, which means he knew the other two would come. However, Harriet Sipper probably didn't know that Ada Gransbury was also staying at Braunsan Hotel, and Ada Gransbury didn't know that Harriet Sipper would come.\" [351] \"Yes, that's possible.\" [352] Encouraged by Poirot, I continued: \"Perhaps the key condition for the killer was that Ada and Harriet didn't know each other was coming to the Brooks Hotel. If that's the case, and Richard Nigel knew both ladies would be staying at the Brooks\u2026\"\n[353] Poirot took over: \"My friend, our thoughts are on the same track. Is it possible that Richard Nigel didn't know he was an accomplice to the killer? The killer may have found a reason to convince Richard Nigel to bring the other two victims to the Brooks Hotel. The question is: is it important that Ada and Harriet didn't know each other would be staying at the same hotel? Is it important for Richard Nigel, the killer, or both?\" [354] \"Maybe Richard Nigel had a plan, and the killer had another plan?\" [355] \"Very likely,\" Poirot said. \"Our next step is to gather as much information as possible about Harriet Spear, Richard Nigel, and Ada Gransbury. Who were they in life? What were their desires, grievances, and secrets? We need to go to the village of Glebeholme to find the answers. Perhaps we can find Jan and the mysterious PIJ there.\" [356] \"I have checked, and there is no guest named Jenny in the hotel. There hasn't been since last night.\" [357] \"Yes, I don't think she's here. The waitress named Phyllis Spurling told me that Jenny lives on the other side of the city. This means she lives in London, not Devonshire, not in Pebble Mill. If Jenny really lives on the 'other side of the city,' she wouldn't need to book a room at Brocks Han Hotel.\" [358] \"By the way, Henry Nigges, Richard's brother, is on his way here from Devonshire. Richard Nigges and his family live together. Moreover, I have arranged a team to investigate the hotel's guests.\" [359] \"Your efficiency is always commendable, Cattupall,\" said Poirot, patting my arm. [360] I felt the need to admit to Poirot that I hadn't done something well. \"The delivery of the room's dinner is a bit of a mystery. I haven't been able to find out who placed the order or who delivered it.\" [361] \"Don't worry,\" Pollock said. \"I will clarify it when we gather at the restaurant later. Now, let's take a walk in the hotel garden. Sometimes, just a casual look can lead to new ideas.\" [362] As soon as we came out, Pollock complained about the weather, and it was indeed getting cloudy. So, I suggested, \"Shall we go back?\" [363] \"No, no, don't go back yet. Changing the environment is good for those little gray cells. Maybe the trees can block the wind. I don't mind the cold, but there's a difference between comfortable and uncomfortable cold, and today's is the latter.\" [364] As soon as we entered the entrance of the Baskerville Hotel garden, we stopped. When I looked at the endless yellow-green grass and the elegant shrubs at the end, I thought that Luca Lazari was not exaggerating. The garden was really beautiful. I saw such an artistic garden for the first time in London. It was not only a transformation of nature, but also a conquest, and it was still pleasing to the eye even in the cold wind. [365] \"What do you think?\" I asked Pollock. \"Shall we go in?\" I thought it would be very pleasant to take a walk on the winding but straight path among the trees. [366] \"I don't know,\" Poirot frowned. \"This weather...\u201d He shivered. [367] \"\u2026It will be colder in the garden, undoubtedly,\" I impatiently finished his sentence. \"But we only have two places to go, Poirot. Either inside the hotel or outside, where do you want to go?\" [368] \"I have a better idea!\" he exclaimed to me. \"Let's take the bus out!\" [369] \"Take the bus? Where to?\" [370] \"Anywhere! It doesn't matter. We'll change buses right away, so we can change environments without freezing. Let's go. We can look out the window and see the city's scenery. Who knows what we might discover.\" He set off decisively. [371] I shook my head as I followed him. \"You're thinking about Jean, aren't you?\" I said, \"We won't see her\u2014\"\n[372] \"It's better than standing here staring at branches and grass!\" Poirot snapped. [373] Ten minutes later, we were on a bus. The windows were covered in fog, and we couldn't see anything, even after wiping them with a handkerchief. [374] I tried to reason with Poirot, \"Speaking of Jean\u2026\"\n[375] \"Hmm?\" [376] \"She might be in danger, but she has nothing to do with the Brownswood Hotel. There's no evidence of any connection. None at all.\" [377] \"I disagree with your assessment, my friend,\" Poirot said sadly. \"I'm becoming more convinced there's a connection.\" [378] \"Really? Why, for heaven's sake, Poirot?\" [379] \"Because there are two peculiar similarities between them.\" [380] \"What are they?\" [381] \"You'll find out, Caderpillar. Really, if you open your mind and think carefully about what you've learned so far, you'll find out.\" [382] Behind us sat an elderly mother and her middle-aged daughter, discussing the difference between ordinary pastry and excellent pastry. [383] \"Caderpillar, did you hear what they said?\" Poirot whispered to me, \"Difference. We can't focus on the similarities; we should focus on the differences \u2013 that's what will guide us to the killer.\" [384] \"What kind of difference?\" I asked Poirot. [385] \"For example, the difference between the third case and the other two. Why were there so many differences in the scene of Richard Nigges' case? Why did the killer lock the door from the inside instead of from the outside? Why did he hide the key behind a loose fireplace tile instead of taking it with him? Why did he climb down from a tree next to the window to leave in that way, instead of walking normally down the corridor? At first, I suspected he might have heard someone talking in the corridor and been afraid to leave Richard Nigges' room without being seen.\" [386] \"That sounds reasonable,\" I said. [387] \"No, I don't think that's the reason.\" [388] \"Oh, why not?\" [389] \"Because the position of the cufflink in Richard Nigges's mouth was different from the other two: completely in the mouth, almost to the throat, not between the lips.\" [390] I complained, \"Here we go again. I really don't think -\"\n[391] \"Ah! Wait, Ketteridge, let's see\u2026\"\n[392] The car stopped, and Poirot stretched his neck, looking at each passenger as they boarded. When the last passenger\u2014a tall, slender man with a suit of tweed and more hair on his ears than on his head\u2014also boarded, Poirot sighed in resignation. [393] \"Because you're disappointed that there's no Jane?\" I said. I had to say it to convince him. [394] \"No, my friend. I am disappointed, but not for the reasons you think. Every time I think of it, I feel disappointed, as it is almost impossible for me to see Jane again in this large city of London. But I remain hopeful.\" [395] \"You always say to follow science, is this not a bit unrealistic now?\" [396] \"Do you think hope is the enemy of science, rather than the driving force? I don't agree with your view, just as I don't agree with your view of the cufflinks. The case of Richard Nigges and the cases of the two other ladies are very different. The position of the cufflinks cannot be explained by the killer hearing someone in the corridor and wanting to avoid them.\" [397] \"Most cases get to this point - of course, it refers to the cases that Heracles Poirot intervenes in - someone doesn't want to participate in the conversation, but wants to think quietly to himself, thinking it's more comfortable, but it's actually ineffective.\" [398] In my mind, facing a reasonable and excellent listener, my silence expresses the following point of view: the slight difference in the position of Richard Nigges' cufflinks is not a critical issue at all. The mouth is the mouth, and any part of the mouth belongs to the mouth. In the killer's eyes, he did the same thing to the three victims: opened their mouths and put a gold cufflink with a combination of letters inside. [399] As for why the killer hid the key behind a loose brick in the fireplace, I haven't come up with a reasonable explanation yet. For the killer, it would be faster and more convenient to take the key away or wipe the fingerprints and throw it on the carpet. [400] The mother and daughter behind us had finished talking about the pastries and started talking about butter. [401] \"We should go back to the hotel,\" Polo said. [402] \"But we just got on the bus!\" I objected. [403] \"Yes, that's right. We can't be too far from the Blaux Han Hotel, as we'll have to go to the restaurant later.\" [404] I slowly exhaled, knowing that there was no need to ask him why in this situation. It was he who came up with the idea in the first place, and now it was he who wanted to go back. [405] \"We have to get off this bus and transfer to another one to go back,\" he said. \"Perhaps the scenery on the next bus will be more beautiful.\" [406] It turned out to be true. Poirot still hadn't seen any sign of Miss Carnaby, which made him anxious, while I enjoyed the interesting scenery and realized once again why I loved London so much. A man dressed as a clown was performing magic tricks, badly, which I had never seen before, but people kept throwing coins into his hat; the most remarkable thing was a dog that looked exactly like a famous politician, and a homeless man sitting on the ground with an open suitcase beside him, eating things out of it as if it were his personal food cart. \"Poirot, look,\" I said. \"That guy doesn't seem to mind the cold, he's so happy, like a cat with cream. No, I mean a homeless man with cream. Poirot, look at that dog, doesn't it remind you of someone? A celebrity. Quick, look, we're passing by.\" [407] \"Carter, get up,\" Poirot said sternly. \"Otherwise, we'll miss our stop. You're always looking around, daydreaming.\" [408] I stood up as soon as we got off the car. \"You brought me out aimlessly wandering in London, so you can't blame me for enjoying the scenery,\" I told him. [409] Poirot suddenly stopped. \"Tell me, why won't you look at the three bodies in the hotel? What makes you unable to bear it?\" [410] \"Nothing. I've looked at them as many times as you have. In fact, I already checked them thoroughly before you came.\" [411] \"If you don't want to talk about it, just say so, my friend.\" [412] \"There's really nothing to talk about. I think that after doing what's necessary, no one would want to stand there and keep looking at the bodies. That's it.\" [413] \"No,\" Poirot said calmly, \"it's not like that.\" [414] I should have told him the reason, but for some reason, I couldn't. When I was five years old, my grandfather died. He was sick for a long time and lay in his room. My parents thought I needed to go to his room every day to visit him because it was important to my grandfather. I didn't want to go, but I went every day to make my parents happy and to make my grandfather happy. I watched his skin turn yellow, listened to his breathing weaken, and watched his eyes lose their shine. I didn't pay attention to these things at the time, and I wasn't afraid. I just remember that every time I was in that room, I counted the seconds until I could stop, leave, and close the door. [415] On the day my grandfather died, I felt like I had been released from prison and was free again. He would soon be taken away, and there would be no more death in the house. But my mother said I had to go to my grandfather's room one last time, and she would come with me, and it would be fine. [416] The doctor laid his body flat, and my mother explained why he did so. I counted the time silently, longer than usual, at least one hundred and thirty seconds. I stood next to my mother, quietly looking at my grandfather's motionless, frail body. Suddenly, my mother said to me, \"Edward, hold his hand.\" I said I didn't want to, and she started crying, tears that seemed to never stop. [417] I had to hold my grandfather's bony hand. I would do anything to let go and escape that room. But I held on, until my mother stopped crying and said we could go downstairs, and then I let go. [418] Hold his hand, Edward, hold his hand. [419] Chapter 5\n[420] The Hundred Person Survey\n[421] When Polo and I walked into the Brawnshan Hotel's restaurant, we hardly noticed the crowd inside.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."